.\" trash-empty(1) manual page for trash-cli.
.TH TRASH-EMPTY 1 2026-11-14 "trash-cli 0.26.9.29" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
trash\-empty \- empty the trash can(s)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B trash\-empty
[\fB\-h\fR]
[\fB\-\-print\-completion {\fIbash\fB,\fIzsh\fB,\fItcsh\fB}\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
[\fB\-v\fR]
[\fB\-\-trash\-dir \fITRASH_DIR\fR]
[\fB\-\-all\-users\fR]
[\fB\-i\fR]
[\fB\-f\fR]
[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
[\fIdays\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B trash\-empty
permanently removes entries from the trash cans; there is no undo.
Without a
.I days
argument everything is removed.
With it, only files trashed strictly before
\fInow \- days\fR
(local time) are removed: a file trashed exactly at the cutoff is kept, and
files with a missing or invalid
.B DeletionDate
are kept.
A negative
.I days
moves the cutoff into the future and so removes everything.
.PP
Per trash can, the backup copies of the matching entries are removed first,
then their info files; afterwards every orphan, an entry of
.I files/
without a matching info file, is removed regardless of
.IR days .
Entries of
.I info/
that do not end in
\fB.trashinfo\fR
are never touched.
An unparsable trashinfo file, one that is not valid UTF\-8 included, is
reported on standard error and skipped.
Removal uses
.BR unlink (2),
falling back to a recursive removal for directories.
A path that cannot be removed is reported as
\fBtrash\-empty: cannot remove \fI<path>\fR
and the run continues; the exit status stays 0.
.PP
\fB\-i\fR
asks once, covering all trash cans together; the prompt line ends with a
space:
.RS
.PP
.nf
Would empty the following trash directories:
    \- <trash\-dir\-1>
    \- <trash\-dir\-2>
Proceed? (y/N)\ 
.fi
.RE
.PP
An answer whose first character is
\fBy\fR
or
\fBY\fR
proceeds; anything else aborts with no deletions and exit status 0.
\fB\-f\fR
never asks; by default the program asks only when standard input is a
terminal.
An end of file at the prompt is reported on standard error and the exit
status is 1.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-completion {\fIbash\fR,\fIzsh\fR,\fItcsh\fR}
Parse the option and exit 0; this implementation prints
Please install shtab firstly! (upstream required the optional shtab
module to emit a script).
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the program name and version, for example
\fBtrash\-empty 0.26.9.29\fR,
and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print
\fBremoving \fI<path>\fR
before each removal.
.TP
.B \-\-trash\-dir \fIDIR\fR
Empty only the given trash can; the option is repeatable.
It replaces discovery completely.
.TP
.B \-\-all\-users
Empty every account's trash cans;
\fB\-\-trash\-dir\fR
is ignored.
.TP
.B \-i
Ask before emptying (see DESCRIPTION).
.TP
.B \-f
Never ask.
When both
\fB\-i\fR
and
\fB\-f\fR
are given, the last one wins.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print
\fBwould remove \fI<path>\fR
instead of deleting; does not suppress the prompt.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-time
Print the current, possibly faked, clock as
\fBYYYY\-MM\-DDTHH:MM:SS\fR
and exit.
Undocumented upstream; mainly for testing and debugging.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I days
Remove only files trashed strictly before
\fInow \- days\fR.
The value must be an integer; a value that is not an integer, and a value
whose cutoff date falls outside the representable date range (years 1..9999),
are usage errors with exit status 2.
.SH TRASH DIRECTORIES
The trash cans processed are the home trash,
\fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash\fR
when
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is set and non\-empty and
\fI$HOME/.local/share/Trash\fR
otherwise, followed by, for every volume
\fI$volume\fR,
\fI$volume\fB/.Trash/\fI$uid\fR
(trust\-checked as in
.BR trash\-list (1))
and
\fI$volume\fB/.Trash\-\fI$uid\fR.
\fBTRASH_VOLUMES\fR
is honored, as in
.BR trash\-list (1).
Nonexistent cans contribute nothing.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a declined prompt and unremovable leftovers.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: a bad option, a
.I days
value that is not an integer or whose cutoff leaves the representable date
range.
.TP
.B 1
An end of file at the confirmation prompt.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Location of the home trash,
\fI$XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash\fR.
Used only when set and non\-empty; an empty value is treated as unset, per
the XDG basedir specification, so the home trash falls back to
\fI$HOME/.local/share/Trash\fR.
A relative value is used verbatim, resolved against the current directory.
.TP
.B HOME
Fallback location of the home trash,
\fI$HOME/.local/share/Trash\fR.
.TP
.B TRASH_VOLUMES
Colon\-separated volume list overriding the mount table; empty items are
dropped.
.TP
.B TRASH_DATE
Fake the clock for the
.I days
comparison and for
\fB\-\-print\-time\fR,
as
\fBYYYY\-MM\-DDTHH:MM:SS\fR.
An invalid value is warned about
(\fBtrash\-empty: invalid TRASH_DATE: \fI<value>\fR
on standard error) and the real clock is used.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/Trash
The home trash, when
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is set and non\-empty.
.TP
.I $HOME/.local/share/Trash
The home trash otherwise.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.PP
.nf
trash\-empty \-\-dry\-run \-v
trash\-empty 30
trash\-empty \-\-trash\-dir /mnt/usb/.Trash\-1000 \-f
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR trash\-put (1),
.BR trash (1),
.BR trash\-list (1),
.BR trash\-restore (1),
.BR trash\-rm (1).
.PP
Implements the FreeDesktop.org Trash Specification,
https://specifications.freedesktop.org/trash\-spec/
